doc:sch-1-sec-270-12 a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "270 10  Slavery-like offences—relevant evidence" ;
    values:kind "section" ;
    values:originalText "(1) For the purposes of proceedings for a slavery-like offence, the trier of fact may have regard to any of the matters covered by subsection (2) in determining whether a person (the alleged victim) ComLaw Authoritative Act C2013A00006  Schedule 1  Criminal Code amendments" ;
    cof:pageNumber "15"^^xsd:integer .

concept:c2013a00006-sch-1-sec-12-2 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "12 Crimes Legislation Amendment (Slavery, Slavery-like Conditions and People"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-1-sec-12-2 ;
    values:originalText "against whom the offence is alleged to have been committed has been coerced, threatened or deceived.  (2) The following matters are covered by this subsection:  (a) the economic relationship between the alleged victim and the alleged offender;  (b) the terms of any written or oral contract or agreement between the alleged victim and the alleged offender;  (c) the personal circumstances of the alleged victim, including but not limited to:  (i) whether he or she is entitled to be in Australia under the Migration Act 1958; and  (ii) his or her ability to speak, write and understand English or another language; and  (iii) the extent of his or her social and physical dependence on the alleged offender.  (3) Subsection (1) does not:  (a) prevent the leading of any other evidence in the relevant proceedings; or  (b) limit the manner in which evidence may be given or the admissibility of evidence. Subdivision D—Offences against Division 270: general" ;
    skos:definition "against whom the offence is alleged to have been committed has been coerced, threatened or deceived.  (2) The following matters are covered by this subsection:  (a) the economic relationship between the alleged victim and the alleged offender;  (b) the terms of any written or oral contract or agreement between the alleged victim and the alleged offender;  (c) the personal circumstances of the alleged victim, including but not limited to:  (i) whether he or she is entitled to be in Australia under the Migration Act 1958; and  (ii) his or her ability to speak, write and understand English or another language; and  (iii) the extent of his or her social and physical dependence on the alleged offender.  (3) Subsection (1) does not:  (a) prevent the leading of any other evidence in the relevant proceedings; or  (b) limit the manner in which evidence may be given or the admissibility of evidence. Subdivision D—Offences against Division 270: general"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "0063d021b15d1fea429c75ead3d7d091a5be554e2dcb19a7f4134d2b9a45fa50" ;
    cof:pageNumber "16"^^xsd:integer ;
